---
description: QuotePilot code quality & maintainability standards
alwaysApply: true
---

# Simplicity first

- Build for the current phase only. No speculative features or "future-proof" abstractions.
- Rule of three: don't extract a helper/abstraction until the same code appears 3 times.
- Prefer the boring, obvious solution. Fewer files, fewer dependencies, less magic.

# Folder structure (keep files in their home)

- app/ → routes & pages (App Router)
- components/ui/ → shadcn/ui primitives
- components/ → feature components
- lib/ → external-service wrappers (ai, email, billing, db) + pure utils
- db/ → Drizzle schema + migrations + seed
- types/ → shared types (prefer inferring from Drizzle)
- Server logic lives in server actions or route handlers, never in client components.

# Naming

- Files: kebab-case (quote-card.tsx). Components: PascalCase. Vars/functions: camelCase.
- DB tables: snake_case, plural. Booleans: is/has/can. Handlers: handleX.
- Names say intent: `acceptQuote`, not `doIt`. No abbreviations that aren't obvious.

# One source of truth

- Derive types from the Drizzle schema; don't hand-write duplicate shapes.
- Put statuses, plan names, quota limits, follow-up days, etc. in `lib/constants.ts`.
  No magic strings or magic numbers scattered in the code.

# Validate at the edges

- Validate ALL input at every API route / server action boundary with Zod.
- Trust nothing from the client (never a client-sent business_id, price, or plan).

# Errors

- Never swallow errors silently. Catch at boundaries.
- User-facing messages are friendly and actionable ("Enter a price" not "500 error").
- Log server errors with enough context to debug. Fail loudly in dev, gracefully in prod.

# Size & readability

- Split a file/function when it passes ~200 lines or does more than one job.
- Comments explain WHY, not WHAT. Delete dead code and commented-out blocks.
- No `console.log` left in committed code (use a logger or remove).

# Consistency

- One way to fetch data, one way to build a form, one way to show a toast — then repeat it.
- Match the patterns already in the codebase before inventing a new one.

# Dependencies

- Add a library only when it clearly saves real work. Pin versions. Avoid abandoned packages.

# Tests (pragmatic, not 100%)

- Write tests for the things that hurt if wrong: quote total math (cents), AI-output
  parsing/validation, and the accept-quote flow. Skip trivial UI tests.

# Cost & abuse control

- Rate-limit public endpoints: the free calculator, the public quote view, and any AI call.
  This protects your OpenAI bill and blocks abuse.
